// THE STATIC TIER'S COLUMN DOMAIN, PINNED AT THE EXTREMES — 20 TYPES × 2
// POSITIONS, ADMISSION AND VALUE TOGETHER.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. A 1066-cell survey (2026-08-03) measured what each of
// the three Deem tiers admits, refuses and answers, per type and per position.
// NONE of it was pinned by the corpus: the 21-row lattice in
// `stdlib/mem/wql/el.logos` could gain or lose a row, a tier could start or
// stop admitting a type, and a value could stop surviving its round trip, with
// every gate green. That is the population-listed-not-derived defect this
// codebase spent two days closing everywhere else, and a survey that is not a
// fixture decays into a paragraph in a report nobody re-runs.
//
// ⚠ WHAT IS AND IS NOT AN ORACLE HERE. Every value below and every expected
// permutation is a CONSTANT written by hand. This file never asks `el_ty`,
// `el_ty_at` or `el_ty_arity` what the lattice holds and never folds an answer
// with the same arithmetic the query compiler folds it with — an oracle that
// shares the algorithm with the code under test agrees with itself by
// construction, which is on file here twice (`feedback_oracle_must_be_independent`,
// and the header of `wql_agg_avg_bool_three_engines`).
//
// ⚠ AND WHY THE EXTREMES RATHER THAN 42. A type that is ADMITTED but whose
// value does not survive is the defect this arc exists to end, and it is
// invisible at small magnitudes: the measured `u64`/`usize` defect in the
// DYNAMIC tier — `order by` answering 2^63 < 2^64-1 < 5 — is exactly correct
// for every value below 2^63 and wrong for every value at or above it. So each
// row set here straddles the boundary that can break it:
//   • every signed type at its own MIN and MAX (i64::MIN is the value whose
//     negation does not exist);
//   • every unsigned type at 0, at 2^(w-1) and at 2^w-1, so a signed compare
//     and an unsigned one give DIFFERENT permutations rather than the same one;
//   • `f32` at 0.1f32, the value that is destroyed by a round trip through an
//     f64 arm read as raw bits, and at ±2^20+0.5, exact in both widths;
//   • `f64` at ±1.0e300, outside f32 entirely.
//
// WHAT IS PINNED, per type: that `select s.x` COMPILES (so the static tier
// admits the type as a column), that it yields `Vec<T>` and not `Vec<i64>` (so
// the column type is preserved rather than collapsed to the class
// representative), that all four values come back bit-identical, and that
// `order by s.x` sorts by the type's OWN order. The row placement is one
// permutation for every type — row 0 = v2, row 1 = v0, row 2 = v3, row 3 = v1 —
// so the ascending answer is the id sequence 1, 3, 0, 2 in all 19 non-bool
// cases, and a tier that sorted by anything other than the column's own `<`
// (e.g. signed at 64 bits over `u64`, which gives 0, 2, 1, 3) says so by
// producing a different sequence, not by refusing.
//
// EXIT CODES: base = 20 + 6*i for the i-th type in declaration order, and
//   +0 `select` returned Err          +3 the HIGH extreme did not survive
//   +1 wrong row count                +4 an interior value did not survive
//   +2 the LOW extreme did not survive +5 `order by` Err/count/permutation
// so a red names the (type, question) cell directly. All codes are ≤ 139: the
// exit status is 8-bit and a code above 255 comes back as its residue.
//
// ⚠ WHAT THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT PIN. It moves no boundary: every cell
// asserted here was measured GREEN, so nothing about what the tier ACCEPTS
// changes. The static tier's REFUSALS live in `fail/` fixtures, because a
// program that does not compile cannot be a pass test — see
// `wql_domain_layer_*_fail` for the refusal-plus-layer half, and
// `wql_domain_runtime_extremes` for the same 20 types put to the dynamic and
// incremental engines, where 6 of these cells answer differently.
//
// exit 0 = all 20 types admitted, all 80 values survived, all 20 orders correct.
package test;

use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;
use logos.lang.result;
use logos.lang.str;

struct S_i8 { pub x: i8, pub id: i64 }
pub deem sel_i8(ss: &[S_i8]) { from ss s select s.x }
pub deem ord_i8(ss: &[S_i8]) { from ss s select s.id order by s.x }

struct S_i16 { pub x: i16, pub id: i64 }
pub deem sel_i16(ss: &[S_i16]) { from ss s select s.x }
pub deem ord_i16(ss: &[S_i16]) { from ss s select s.id order by s.x }

struct S_i32 { pub x: i32, pub id: i64 }
pub deem sel_i32(ss: &[S_i32]) { from ss s select s.x }
pub deem ord_i32(ss: &[S_i32]) { from ss s select s.id order by s.x }

